---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import Questions from "@daviddef/archive-kit/components/Questions.astro";
import Rule from "../components/Rule.astro";
import gaps from "../data/gaps.json";
import { emph } from "../lib/text.js";
import qs from "../data/open-questions.json";

/* THE QUESTIONS ARE ROWS NOW, not prose - extracted 7 October 2026 into
   data/open-questions.tsv. The kit's Questions component says in its own
   docstring that five of seven archives keep their questions as prose and
   "cannot use this until somebody extracts them, and that extraction is
   research work belonging to each archive rather than something to guess at
   from outside". This is that extraction, done from inside.

   WHAT HAD TO SURVIVE IT: the long panel under question 1 - the Lerena-Lerena
   compound test, the forty-three transcription doublings, the Carmen Lerena
   act read off the plate, and the line that says the test lowers the
   probability without settling it. All of it is in that row's `tried`. A
   refactor that loses a question's negative evidence is worse than the prose
   it replaced. */
const rows = qs.filter((r) => r.q);
/* 9 October 2026. «What we cannot close» folded in here. It said so itself, in its
   own words: "not the same page as the open questions", the places where the work
   has been done properly and the record may simply not exist. That is a STATE of
   a question, not a separate subject, and a reader looking for what this archive
   does not know had to find two pages. /gaps/ redirects to #may-never-close. The
   count is derived from the data, so the sentence cannot go stale. */
const NUM = ["no", "one", "two", "three", "four", "five", "six", "seven", "eight", "nine", "ten", "eleven", "twelve"];
const word = (n) => (n < NUM.length ? NUM[n] : String(n));
---
<Base title="Open Questions"
      eyebrow="Published so strangers can help"
      dek="Published deliberately. An archive that lists only its answers is advertising; one that lists its questions can be helped by strangers.">

<section class="blk">
  <div class="prose">
    <p><strong>Seven questions and two closed doors.</strong> Each carries what is known, what has
      already been tried, and &mdash; the part that makes this a work queue rather than a list of
      regrets &mdash; <strong>the specific record that would answer it</strong>.</p>
    <p class="cap">The two marked <em>blocked</em> are not questions about the family. They are routes
      that failed, kept so nobody walks them again.</p>
    <p class="cap">And {word(gaps.length)} more <strong>may never close</strong>: the work has been done
      properly and the record may simply not exist. <a href="#may-never-close">Those are below &darr;</a></p>
  </div>
  <Questions rows={rows} u={u} unit="questions" />
</section>

<Rule name="arrows" label="Unfinished, and possibly unfinishable" />

<section class="blk" id="may-never-close">
  <h2>What we cannot close</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>An archive that lists only its questions implies they are all answerable. Some are not. A parish
      register with a gap in it, a marriage in a country nobody has narrowed, a farm held on a lease that
      left no title &mdash; these do not yield to persistence.</p>
    <p><strong>Each row says what was actually done</strong>, so that nobody repeats it, and then says
      plainly what would have to exist for the gap to be closable at all.</p>
  </div>
  {gaps.map(r => (
    <div class="panel" style="margin-top:20px">
      <p style="margin:0;font-size:1.08rem"><strong set:html={emph(r.gap)} /></p>
      <p style="margin:12px 0 0"><span class="lab">What has been done</span><br /><span set:html={emph(r.what_has_been_done)} /></p>
      <p style="margin:12px 0 0"><span class="lab">Why it may never close</span><br /><span set:html={emph(r.why_it_may_never_close)} /></p>
    </div>
  ))}
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:20px">These are distinct from the open questions above, which
    are answerable and merely unanswered, and from the <a href={u("/hypotheses/")}>hypotheses</a>, which
    are propositions this archive is entertaining without having proved.</div>
</section>

</Base>
